Sval: helper executables take names of Sval's own

killall and taskkill pick processes by name, and pkill by any part of one.
Firefox's helpers have fixed names, so a command meant for an installed
Firefox's crash helper, child processes or updater also reached Sval's. Each
helper's name comes from MOZ_APP_NAME in toolkit/moz.configure: Firefox keeps
Mozilla's names, which an artifact build on Mozilla's packages looks for, and
Sval's helpers are sval-<role>, a name that contains no Firefox helper's name.
Every consumer reads the configured name: the build, the package manifest,
the installer and uninstaller, the artifact code, the code that launches each
helper, and the harnesses that find them.

pingsender and nmhproxy keep their names. Sval never starts either: telemetry
upload is locked off, and the Firefox Bridge that registers nmhproxy is off.

